headers {
  Content-Type: application/json
}

auth {
  mode: none
}

script:pre-request {
  // Linear expects the personal API key as a BARE `Authorization: <key>` value with no
  // `Bearer` prefix — this mirrors source_linear/resources.py:11-29, where the CDK
  // TokenSource is built with authorization_token_type="".
  //
  // The decrypted key lives on `req` for this one in-flight request only. Never
  // bru.setVar / bru.setEnvVar it: that would persist the secret into a committed file.
  const { execFileSync } = require('child_process');

  const configPath = bru.getEnvVar('config_path');
  if (!configPath) throw new Error('config_path env var is not set');

  const decrypted = execFileSync(
    'sops', ['-d', '--output-type=json', configPath], { encoding: 'utf8' },
  );
  const token = JSON.parse(decrypted)?.credentials?.access_token_sops;
  if (!token) throw new Error('credentials.access_token_sops missing from decrypted config');

  req.setHeader('Authorization', token);
}

script:post-response {
  // Linear meters two independent budgets and reports both on EVERY response, including
  // errors. Echo them so each run records what the query actually cost — `X-Complexity`
  // is the number the 10,000-point per-query cap is measured against, and it is the only
  // way to size `first` per stream.
  //
  // No body redaction is configured. Linear returns no HATEOAS envelope and no volatile
  // per-request ids, and the fields that do churn (`updatedAt`, cursors) are exactly the
  // ones these probes exist to observe. If snapshot tests later redact fields, mirror
  // that list here and cross-reference it from the test's REDACTED_FIELDS.
  const h = res.getHeaders();
  console.log(JSON.stringify({
    status: res.getStatus(),
    complexity: h['x-complexity'],
    requests_remaining: h['x-ratelimit-requests-remaining'],
    requests_limit: h['x-ratelimit-requests-limit'],
    complexity_remaining: h['x-ratelimit-complexity-remaining'],
    complexity_limit: h['x-ratelimit-complexity-limit'],
    complexity_reset: h['x-ratelimit-complexity-reset'],
  }));
}

docs {
  Bruno collection for `source-linear`, verifying the Issues / Projects / Initiatives /
  Labels cluster against the live Linear GraphQL API.

  Reference: https://linear.app/developers/graphql

  Linear exposes a SINGLE GraphQL endpoint (`https://api.linear.app/graphql`). Every
  request in this collection POSTs a different query document to that same URL; there
  are no per-resource REST paths.

  ## API constraints (account-wide)

  All figures below were confirmed live against the Estuary workspace on 2026-07-30
  unless marked otherwise.

  - **Two independent hourly budgets, both metered per user.** Requests and complexity
    points are counted separately; exhausting either one rate-limits you.
    - Requests: `X-RateLimit-Requests-Limit` / `-Remaining` / `-Reset`.
    - Complexity: `X-RateLimit-Complexity-Limit` / `-Remaining` / `-Reset`.
  - **The request budget is 2,500/hour, NOT 5,000.** Observed live on every response from
    this account: `X-RateLimit-Requests-Limit: 2500`. The rate-limiting docs page leads
    with "up to 5,000 requests per hour" for API-key auth but its own table says 2,500 per
    user per hour; the table is right. Budget against **2,500**. The complexity ceiling
    does match the docs: `X-RateLimit-Complexity-Limit: 3000000`.
  - **Complexity is effectively a non-constraint; REQUESTS are the binding budget.** With
    the connector's real selection sets at `first: 250`, one full page costs at most ~2,225
    points (Projects — the priciest of the four). Exhausting 3,000,000 points/hour would
    take ~1,348 such pages, but the 2,500-request ceiling stops you first. Pace the
    connector on requests/hour and treat complexity as a guardrail, not a scheduler input.
  - **Reset headers are UTC epoch MILLISECONDS**, not seconds and not a delta. Dividing
    by 1000 before converting is required; treating the value as seconds yields a
    timestamp in 1970.
  - **Hard per-query complexity cap of 10,000 points.** Quoting
    https://linear.app/developers/rate-limiting: "We also enforce a maximum complexity
    of a single query at any time to 10,000 points."
  - **The documented complexity formula is NOT what the API charges.** Docs say cost is
    static — "Each property is 0.1 point, each object is 1 point and any connection
    multiplies its children's points based on the given pagination argument, or the default
    50." Measured behavior diverges per root field, which matters because it decides
    whether page size can be sized up front:
    - `projects`, `initiatives`, `issueLabels` — cost scales LINEARLY with the requested
      `first` and is INDEPENDENT of rows returned. `projects(first: N) { nodes { id } }`
      measured 2 / 10 / 50 points at N = 10 / 50 / 250 while returning ZERO rows. Cost here
      is static and therefore predictable in production.
    - `issues` — cost does NOT scale with `first` at all. The same query measured a flat
      2 points at N = 10, 50 and 250. Even a deliberately absurd
      `issues(first: 250) { nodes { comments(first: 250) { … } } }` — ~87,750 points under
      the documented formula — returned HTTP 200 at `X-Complexity: 6` (see
      `05 - Complexity Envelope`). The 10,000 cap is evidently enforced against actual
      executed cost for this field, not the static estimate.
    Consequence: the three static-cost streams can be sized with confidence, but `issues`
    cost cannot be predicted from this workspace and the connector should degrade
    gracefully (halve `first`, retry) if it ever meets a complexity rejection in the wild.
  - **Measured cost of the connector's REAL selection sets at `first: 250`** (see each
    stream's `x0 - … Bare` request): Issues **10**, Projects **2,225**, Initiatives
    **1,801**, Labels **1,000**. All four sit inside the 10,000-point cap, so
    **`first: 250` — the maximum — is safe for every stream in this cluster.**
  - **A rate-limited response is HTTP 400, NOT HTTP 429.** The body carries a top-level
    `errors` array whose entry has `extensions.code == "RATELIMITED"`. This is the single
    most important constraint for the connector: the CDK's default retry logic treats
    4xx-other-than-429 as terminal, so a `RATELIMITED` 400 must be detected by INSPECTING
    THE RESPONSE BODY and retried explicitly. Status code alone is not sufficient.
    UNVERIFIED: not reproduced live — provoking it would mean burning the account's full
    2,500-request hourly budget, which is out of proportion to the evidence gained. Taken
    from the docs. The surrounding behavior IS verified, and it corroborates the claim:
    HTTP 400 is demonstrably a generic error channel here, not a rate-limit signal (see
    `42 - Labels Sort Rejected`, a permanent schema error returned as 400).
  - **HTTP status carries NO reliable success/failure signal in either direction.**
    Verified live, and worse than the usual GraphQL caveat — Linear splits failures across
    both status codes with no discernible rule:
    - `06 - Page Size Over Limit`: `first: 251` -> **HTTP 200**, `data: null`,
      `errors[0].extensions.code == "INVALID_INPUT"`. A hard rejection served as a 200.
    - `42 - Labels Sort Rejected`: unknown argument -> **HTTP 400**,
      `errors[0].extensions.code == "GRAPHQL_VALIDATION_FAILED"`.
    So a 200 may be a total failure, and a 400 may be permanent (schema) or transient
    (rate limit). **`extensions.code` is the only reliable discriminator**; every response
    must be parsed for a top-level `errors` array regardless of status. A connector that
    branches on status alone would read the `first: 251` failure as an empty page and
    silently declare the stream exhausted.
  - **Archived records are hidden by default.** Quoting
    https://linear.app/developers/graphql: "Archived resources are hidden by default from
    the paginated responses. They can be included by passing optional `includeArchived:
    true` as a query parameter for pagination." Omitting it silently drops a partition —
    see the `includeArchived` probes per stream.
  - **Default page size is 50; MAXIMUM is 250.** The docs state the default ("The first 50
    results are returned by default without query arguments") but never the ceiling. Found
    live: `first: 251` is rejected with `first must not be greater than 250` — rejected,
    not clamped, and `data` comes back null. The connector must clamp to <= 250 itself.
    See `06 - Page Size Over Limit`.
  - **`orderBy` is DESCENDING-only, for both `createdAt` and `updatedAt`.** Undocumented in
    the prose docs, and the `PaginationOrderBy` enum values carry null descriptions in the
    schema. Established live in `04 - OrderBy Direction Probe`. Three of the four cluster
    root fields (`issues`, `projects`, `initiatives`) also accept a richer
    `sort: [<Entity>SortInput!]` argument with an explicit
    `order: Ascending | Descending`; `issueLabels` does NOT (`42 - Labels Sort Rejected`).
    Ascending order is what allows safe per-page cursor checkpointing, so this asymmetry
    drives the per-stream checkpoint policy.
  - **Pagination cursors are keyset values derived from the document, not opaque server
    tokens** — despite one of the two encodings looking opaque. Under `orderBy`,
    `pageInfo.endCursor` is the last node's raw UUID. Under `sort`, it is a base64 blob
    that decodes to `{"key": "<last node uuid>", "groups": [...]}`. Neither holds
    server-side state or has a lifetime, so neither carries the durability risk of a true
    continuation token. The connector nonetheless persists only `updatedAt` between sync
    cycles and uses `after` strictly within a single sweep.
  - **`updatedAt` ties are common and are the cluster's main correctness hazard.** Bulk
    operations stamp identical `updatedAt` across many rows — in this workspace all 4
    issues, all 3 labels and all 6 workflow states share one millisecond. Verified in
    `07 - Cursor Filter Boundary`: `gt` is strictly exclusive and `gte` inclusive at 1 ms
    resolution, so a cursor landing inside a tie group either skips the remainder (`gt`) or
    re-reads it forever (`gte`). Cursor policy must address this explicitly.
}
